ing Turner up 7-8 points", "Consensus of expert raters at Likely R, with markets pricing Turner near 83%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable but not safe Republican hold: Turner is a well-funded incumbent in an R+4 district, the only field polls (Democratic-sponsored, from July) show him ahead by 7-8, and nine of ten raters call the race Likely R or better. A modest Democratic environment and Knickerbocker's credible fundraising keep this short of Safe R.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling or a major national environment shift toward Democrats could move this toward Tossup, given the polls are months old.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 76 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Turner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Mike Turner seeks another term in this Dayton-area seat that leans Republican by about four points. He holds a large financial advantage, with roughly $556,000 on hand to Knickerbocker's $125,000, and no significant outside spending has entered the race. A Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot.\n\nThe available polling, all Democratic-sponsored and conducted in July, showed Turner leading by seven to eight points. Expert raters overwhelmingly place the race in the Likely Republican column, though one calls it Lean R and another Safe R, and prediction markets give Turner about an 83 percent chance. The forecast expects Turner to win by roughly seven points, with Knickerbocker needing an unusually favorable national environment to close the gap.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1787, "margin": -5.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1387, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1203, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1216, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.119, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1452, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_